      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "We assessed the relationship between height and coronary heart disease (CHD) in an urban population of Tehran."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "4110 participants of the Tehran Lipid and Glucose Study who were 40 years of age or older (1880 men and 2230 women; mean age, 55.1 and 53.0 years, respectively) and free of CHD at baseline were followed for a mean of 9.1 years. We used Cox proportional hazards regression to evaluate the risk of a first CHD event across height tertiles."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "First CHD events occurred in 239 men and 172 women. The estimated crude HR (95% CI) for CHD events associated with an increment of 1 SD in height was 0.96 (0.28-3.33) in men and 0.84 (0.72-0.97) in women. After adjustment for age, the associations were no longer present. Further adjustment for other confounders had little impact on the results: the HR (95% CI) associated with an increase of 1 SD in height was 1.02 (0.87-1.20) in men and 0.82 (0.66-1.02) in women."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "After adjustment for age, height was not associated with CHD incidence in men or women."
        }
      ]},
